-- Insurance claim settlement - paying out on claims without ever
-- letting the total quietly slip past the cap the policy promised.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case insurance is not your world.
-- When a business buys an insurance policy, the policy carries an
-- *aggregate limit*: the most the insurer will pay out across every
-- claim on that policy for the period - say 100,000 in total. A single
-- storm might produce one claim; a bad year might produce several. Each
-- time the insurer settles a claim it pays out some money, and the sum
-- of all those payments must never exceed the aggregate. A claims
-- handler does the settling, but only up to their own delegated
-- authority - they can sign off payments, but not unlimited ones. Months
-- later a regulator may ask: who authorised this payment, under what
-- authority, on what day, and was the policy still within its limit when
-- the decision was made? The answer should be one lookup, not a forensic
-- reconciliation across three separate systems.
--
-- The hard part is the *cumulative* nature of the cap. No single payment
-- is obviously too large; it is the running total that must stay in
-- bounds. The first payment of 60,000 is fine. A later one of 40,000 is
-- fine too - it brings the total to exactly 100,000. But a third payment
-- of 30,000 would push the total to 130,000, and that one must be
-- refused outright: no money staged, no record written, because nothing
-- was admitted.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about it. The system never stores bare
-- "facts" - it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey
-- the rules. Two distinct kinds of rule guard the cap, and the split
-- between them is the main lesson here:
--
--   * A `require` is a gate on a single action, checked the moment you
--     act. Before authorising a payment, the gate adds up everything
--     already paid on the policy plus the proposed amount and refuses if
--     that would breach the aggregate. This is the "am I allowed to do
--     this?" question a handler interacts with.
--   * An `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     and the runtime refuses to commit any change that would break it.
--     We also track the policy's *remaining headroom* as a running
--     counter, and an invariant insists that every payment must drop
--     that headroom by exactly its own amount. This is the "did I
--     actually do it correctly?" question. A buggy action that recorded
--     a payment but forgot to lower the headroom would pass the gate and
--     still be caught by the invariant.
--
-- Notice what is deliberately *not* a rule here: there is no standing
-- rule tying a recorded authorisation to live authority. Revoking a
-- handler's authority tomorrow must not invalidate the payments they
-- lawfully authorised today. Authority is checked once, at the gate; the
-- record then stands.

program insurance_claim_settlement

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold. `Policy` and its aggregate cap are set once and never change.
-- `PolicyHeadroom` is the one moving part - the remaining capacity
-- counter, retracted and re-admitted as each payment lands.
-- `SettlementAuthority` is a handler's personal ceiling. The last three
-- are the durable record: what was reported, who authorised what, and
-- what was paid. `PolicyLimitUsage` is a read-only reporting view (see
-- the bottom of the file).
-- The uniqueness each unique-lookup gate depends on is declared on
-- the predicates themselves: one policy per id, one report per claim
-- id, one headroom counter per policy, one payment per settlement id,
-- one set of coverage terms per policy. The runtime enforces each as
-- a generated invariant you can read in `inspect guarantees`.
predicate Policy(policy_id: Subject, aggregate_limit: Decimal)
    unique by (policy_id)
predicate ClaimReported(claim_id: Subject, policy_id: Subject, claimed_amount: Decimal)
    unique by (claim_id)
predicate SettlementAuthority(actor: Subject, limit: Decimal)
predicate SettlementAuthorised(claim_id: Subject, settlement_id: Subject, amount: Decimal, actor: Subject)
predicate SettlementPaid(policy_id: Subject, claim_id: Subject, settlement_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
    unique by (settlement_id)
predicate PolicyHeadroom(policy_id: Subject, remaining: Decimal)
    unique by (policy_id)
predicate PolicyLimitUsage(policy_id: Subject, used: Decimal)
-- An optional per-claim layer that sits inside the aggregate cap: a
-- deductible the insured bears, and a per-claim limit. When set, a claim
-- pays the loss above the deductible, capped at the per-claim limit.
predicate CoverageTerms(policy_id: Subject, deductible: Decimal, per_claim_limit: Decimal)
    unique by (policy_id)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - a policy was issued, a claim reported, a payment
-- requested. The payment-request intent is how a finance system
-- downstream learns it should actually move the money.
intent PolicyIssued(policy_id: Subject)
intent ClaimReported(claim_id: Subject)
intent SettlementAuthorityGranted(principal: Subject, limit: Decimal)
intent ClaimPaymentRequested(settlement_id: Subject, amount: Decimal, actor: Subject)
intent CoverageTermsSet(policy_id: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. Any change that would leave the
-- world violating one of these is rejected outright.

-- No payment may exist without a matching authorisation behind it. A
-- hand-constructed payment with no one's sign-off is refused. Read
-- `implies` as "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".
invariant paid_implies_authorised:
    SettlementPaid(_, c, s, a) implies SettlementAuthorised(c, s, a, _)

-- Every payment must sit against a policy that has a current headroom
-- counter. This closes a loophole in the conservation rule below, which
-- would otherwise be trivially satisfied if no headroom counter existed
-- at all.
invariant paid_implies_headroom:
    SettlementPaid(p, _, _, _) implies (exists r: PolicyHeadroom(p, r))

-- The conservation law. This is the rule that compares the policy's
-- state *before* a transaction to its state after: the headroom must go
-- down by exactly the total of the payments newly admitted in that same
-- transaction. `pre(...)` refers to a value as it stood before the
-- change. Summing the new payments and comparing once (rather than
-- payment by payment) is what keeps it correct even if two payments land
-- together. Pairs with the gate inside authorise_settlement: the gate
-- decides the payment is allowed, this rule confirms the books actually
-- moved by the right amount.
invariant headroom_consumed_by_payment:
    PolicyHeadroom(p, after) and pre(PolicyHeadroom(p, before))
    implies after = before - sum(amt | SettlementPaid(p, _, s, amt) and not pre(SettlementPaid(p, _, s, amt)))

-- The per-claim layer, when coverage terms are set for the policy. A
-- settlement may never exceed the *eligible payout*: the loss above the
-- deductible, capped at the per-claim limit. The `let` names that first
-- piece - max(0, ...) floors a below-deductible loss at zero, so the
-- insurer never owes a negative amount - and the min(...) then caps the
-- covered loss at the per-claim limit. A `let` is an abbreviation, not
-- a step that runs first: `loss` and `deductible` get their meaning
-- where `covered_loss` is used, after the claims below have matched
-- them. A policy with no coverage terms
-- set is unaffected - the rule is vacuous without a CoverageTerms claim
-- - so this layer composes with, and sits inside, the aggregate cap
-- above.
invariant settlement_within_eligible_payout:
    let covered_loss = (max(0, loss - deductible))
    SettlementAuthorised(claim_id, _, amount, _)
    and ClaimReported(claim_id, policy_id, loss)
    and CoverageTerms(policy_id, deductible, per_claim_limit)
    implies amount <= min(per_claim_limit, covered_loss)

-- Coverage terms must make business sense: a deductible is non-negative
-- (zero means no deductible), and a per-claim limit is strictly positive
-- (a zero or negative limit would make the eligible-payout rule
-- nonsensical - no claim could ever pay out).
invariant coverage_terms_within_range:
    CoverageTerms(p, deductible, per_claim_limit)
    implies (0 <= deductible and 0 < per_claim_limit)



-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every rule above still holds
-- once they are in place.

-- Open a policy. This records the contractual cap (set once, never
-- touched again) and starts the headroom counter at the full aggregate,
-- since nothing has been paid yet.
transformation issue_policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit):
    admit Policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    admit PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    emit PolicyIssued(policy_id)

-- Record a reported loss against a policy. The gate insists the policy
-- exists first. The claimed amount is informational - it does not cap
-- the settlement; the aggregate cap is enforced from the payments
-- actually made.
transformation report_claim(claim_id, policy_id, claimed_amount):
    require Policy(policy_id, _)
    admit ClaimReported(claim_id, policy_id, claimed_amount)
    emit ClaimReported(claim_id)

-- Grant a claims handler their personal settlement ceiling.
transformation grant_settlement_authority(principal, limit):
    admit SettlementAuthority(principal, limit)
    emit SettlementAuthorityGranted(principal, limit)

-- Set the per-claim coverage layer for a policy: a deductible and a
-- per-claim limit. Optional - a policy without it has only the aggregate
-- cap. The gate insists the policy exists and that terms are not already
-- set. Once set, the eligible-payout invariant above bounds every
-- settlement on the policy's claims.
transformation set_coverage_terms(policy_id, deductible, per_claim_limit):
    require Policy(policy_id, _)
    require not CoverageTerms(policy_id, _, _)
    admit CoverageTerms(policy_id, deductible, per_claim_limit)
    emit CoverageTermsSet(policy_id)

-- Authorise a settlement against a reported claim - the heart of the
-- example. It first looks up the claim, its policy, and the policy's
-- current headroom. Then two gates must pass: the proposing actor must
-- hold authority covering the amount, and the cumulative spend (all
-- prior payments plus this one) must stay within the aggregate cap. Only
-- then does it lower the headroom by the amount, record who authorised
-- what and what was paid, and ask downstream to move the money. The
-- conservation invariant above checks the headroom move was honest.
--
-- Notice the names in front of the colons. Five things here can refuse a
-- settlement - three lookups that must find their record, two conditions
-- that must hold - and without names a refusal could only quote back the
-- expression that failed. Quoted text reads well and identifies nothing:
-- reword the condition and anything relying on it breaks. A name is the
-- author's own label, and it survives any rewording of the rule beneath
-- it, so a test, a runbook, or a report grouping refusals by cause can
-- hold on to it. Naming is optional, and worth it exactly here, where
-- several rules could each be the one that said no.
transformation authorise_settlement(claim_id, settlement_id, amount):
    bind the_claim: ClaimReported(claim_id, policy_id, _)
    bind the_policy: Policy(policy_id, aggregate_limit)
    bind the_headroom: PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, current_headroom)

    require actor_has_authority_for_amount: SettlementAuthority(actor, actor_limit) and amount <= actor_limit
    require cumulative_spend_within_aggregate_cap: sum(paid | SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, paid)) + amount <= aggregate_limit

    let new_headroom = current_headroom - amount
    retract PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, current_headroom)
    admit PolicyHeadroom(policy_id, new_headroom)

    admit SettlementAuthorised(claim_id, settlement_id, amount, actor)
    admit SettlementPaid(policy_id, claim_id, settlement_id, amount)
    emit ClaimPaymentRequested(settlement_id, amount, actor)


-- A reporting view: total paid on each policy, summed from the payment
-- records whenever someone asks. This is a *derived* claim - read-only,
-- recomputed on demand, never stored on the side - so it can never drift
-- away from the payments it is built from. Distinct from the headroom
-- counter, which is live operational state; this view can be replayed
-- as it stood at any past moment to answer "what was the position then?".
derived PolicyLimitUsage(policy_id):
    over SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, _)
    value used = sum(paid | SettlementPaid(policy_id, _, _, paid))
